! Silicon with one atom pushed off its site, asked for its stress tensor.
!
! The norm-conserving LDA reference case for P11. It is `si2-nc-force.in` with
! `tstress = .true.` added, deliberately: the force and the stress are the same
! construction along two different coordinates, so validating them on the *same*
! state is what makes a disagreement attributable to the coordinate rather than
! to the system.
!
! The displaced atom matters here for a reason it did not there. On the ideal
! crystal the stress is isotropic by symmetry and a tensor that is wrong in its
! shear components looks perfect; moving the second atom by 0.02 alat along x
! leaves 8 of the 48 operations, and the surviving group allows a genuinely
! anisotropic tensor with nonzero off-diagonal entries.
!
! conv_thr is tightened far past what the answer needs for the reason the force
! inputs give: QE's stress is evaluated on a density that has stopped where
! conv_thr told it to, and at 1e-10 that stopping point costs less than the
! agreement being claimed.
!
! verbosity = 'high' makes pw.x print the tensor term by term, in kbar, which is
! what the term-by-term comparison in tests/regression/test_stress.py reads.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
    tstress = .true.
    tprnfor = .true.
    verbosity = 'high'
 /
 &system
    ibrav=2, celldm(1)=10.20, nat=2, ntyp=1,
    ecutwfc=12.0
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Si  28.086  Si.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Si 0.00 0.00 0.00
 Si 0.27 0.25 0.25
K_POINTS (automatic)
 4 4 4 0 0 0
